questions.explanation:
Without `await`, you get the **Promise object**, not its resolved value:

```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  // ✗ Missing await — returns Promise, not user
  const user = fetchUser(id)
  console.log(user) // Promise { <pending> }
  console.log(user.name) // undefined!

  // Errors from fetchUser() become unhandled rejections
}

async function getUser(id) {
  // ✓ Correct
  const user = await fetchUser(id)
  console.log(user.name) // works
}
```

Typescript (`@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises`) and ESLint rules can catch this. Watch for it especially in loops and conditional branches.

`Promise.resolve(value)` returns a Promise that is already fulfilled with `value`. If `value` is itself a Promise (or thenable), it returns that Promise unchanged.

**Use cases:**

```javascript
// Normalize sync/async return values:
function maybeAsync(val) {
  return Promise.resolve(val) // always a Promise
}

// Useful in tests:
const mockFetch = jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: 1, name: 'Alice' })
// equivalent to: mockFetch.mockReturnValue(Promise.resolve(...))

// Short-circuit a cache hit:
async function getData(key) {
  if (cache.has(key)) return Promise.resolve(cache.get(key))
  const data = await fetchFromDb(key)
  cache.set(key, data)
  return data
}
```

A single `catch` catches errors from any `await` in the `try` block — you lose granularity:

```javascript
// ✗ Unclear which operation failed:
try {
  const user = await getUser(id)
  const posts = await getPosts(user.id)
  await savePosts(posts)
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Something failed:', err) // which step?
}

// ✓ Granular error handling:
let user
try { user = await getUser(id) }
catch (err) { throw new Error(`Failed to get user: ${err.message}`) }

let posts
try { posts = await getPosts(user.id) }
catch (err) { throw new Error(`Failed to get posts: ${err.message}`) }
```

Alternatively, wrap individual awaits in small functions with specific error handling.

`AbortController` (available since Node.js 15) provides a signal that can be used to cancel operations:

```javascript
const controller = new AbortController()
const { signal } = controller

// Cancel after 5 seconds
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000)

try {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data', { signal })
  const data = await res.json()
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
    console.log('Request was cancelled')
  }
}
```

Also works with `fs.readFile`, `dns.lookup`, and other Node.js APIs that accept a `signal` option.

